---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  ogType="article"
  jsonLd={[articleJsonLd, faqJsonLd]}
  author="World Battery Hub"
  dateModified="2026-09-22"
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="byline">Procurement · Warranty Terms · Last reviewed 2026-09-22</p>
      <h1>Battery Warranty Terms for Distributors</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        The headline "36-month warranty" is the least informative sentence on the quotation. What the
        distributor actually buys is a bundle of clauses — free-replacement window, pro-rata rate, exclusions,
        freight rules and a claims process — and the bundle, not the number, determines what a warranty is
        worth.
      </p>

      <div class="takeaways">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> Read the warranty as a <strong>cost and liability instrument</strong>, not a
        durability promise. Compare the <strong>free-replacement window</strong>, the <strong>pro-rata rate</strong>, the
        <strong>exclusions</strong> and the <strong>freight terms</strong> across suppliers — warranty length alone
        compares nothing. The consumer-side basics are covered in
        <a href="/lead-acid/battery-warranty/">how battery warranties work</a>.
      </div>

      <h2>The two warranty structures: free replacement vs pro-rata</h2>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th></th>
            <th>Free replacement</th>
            <th>Pro-rata</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>What the buyer receives</strong></td>
            <td>A replacement battery at no charge (freight terms separate)</td>
            <td>Credit proportional to the time remaining on the stated period</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>When it applies</strong></td>
            <td>Early life — the opening window of the warranty</td>
            <td>Later life — after the free window closes, to the end of the period</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Risk to the distributor</strong></td>
            <td>Stock and freight obligation, in full</td>
            <td>Partial credit that shrinks with battery age</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        Most battery warranties combine both: a free-replacement window (commonly the opening months of the
        period) followed by a pro-rata tail. The split matters more than the total. A "24-month warranty" that
        is free replacement for 6 months and pro-rata after, is a different instrument from a "24-month" that
        is free for 18 — and both are different from a "36-month" that is pro-rata from day one.
      </p>

      <h2>What voids a warranty — the exclusions</h2>
      <p>
        Claim disputes concentrate in the exclusions, not the length. Typical exclusion language covers:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Physical damage</strong> — dropped, crushed, or post-failure damage of any kind.</li>
        <li><strong>Over-discharge and deep cycling</strong> — using a starting battery for deep-cycle duty, or leaving it discharged. See <a href="/lead-acid/deep-cycle-vs-starting/">starting vs deep-cycle</a> for the duty classes.</li>
        <li><strong>Wrong charging</strong> — incorrect voltage profile or charger mismatch. See <a href="/lead-acid/agm-charging/">AGM charging requirements</a> for how easily a wrong charger damages a battery.</li>
        <li><strong>Modification</strong> — opened cases, added acid, replaced terminals.</li>
        <li><strong>Application mismatch</strong> — the battery installed outside its design class, vibration or temperature envelope.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        For a distributor, the exclusions define the <em>claim rejection surface</em> — the set of customer failures
        the supplier will refuse. A supplier with a long period and broad exclusions is offering less than one
        with a shorter period and narrow ones, so the two documents must be read side by side.
      </p>

      <h2>Freight, duty and the claims process</h2>
      <p>
        Battery warranty claims carry a logistics cost that is easy to under-price: batteries are heavy,
        hazardous-classed cargo, so return freight, packaging and (on cross-border claims) duty treatment are
        real money on every claim. The written terms must state <strong>who pays return freight, who pays the
        replacement's freight, and who administers the claim</strong> — inspection, decision and credit issuance.
        A warranty that is silent on freight is a warranty that will be renegotiated on the first claim.
      </p>

      <h2>Costing a warranty into the purchase decision</h2>
      <p>
        The disciplined way to compare warranties across a shortlist is to estimate <strong>expected claim cost per
        unit sold</strong>, using the supplier's own terms: free-replacement window × expected early-failure rate,
        plus pro-rata tail × expected later-failure rate, plus freight and administration per claim. The
        failure-rate inputs come from the supplier's claim history and your own market data — not from the
        brochure. Where a supplier cannot or will not share claim-rate history, that is itself a datapoint to
        weigh, alongside the technical evidence in the
        <a href="https://manufacturer.worldbatteryhub.com/verification-criteria/">manufacturer verification criteria</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="muted">
        World Battery Hub publishes no warranty-rate statistics of its own; where a number would be needed,
        this guide names the input and its source discipline instead of inventing one.
      </p>

      <h2>Warranty vs service life — the distinction that prevents claims</h2>
      <p>
        A warranty is a contractual period, not a prediction of life. A battery's real service life is set by
        chemistry, climate, duty and maintenance — see <a href="/lead-acid/how-long-does-a-car-battery-last/">how long a car battery lasts</a>
        and <a href="/lead-acid/heat-degradation/">heat degradation</a> for the physical drivers. Distributors who set
        customer expectations with the warranty document alone inherit the gap between contract and physics;
        those who publish honest expected-life guidance next to the warranty convert a liability into a
        selling point.
      </p>

      <h2>What procurement buyers should check</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>What is the free-replacement window versus the pro-rata tail — and what is the pro-rata credit rate?</li>
        <li>Which failures are excluded, and how broad is the exclusion language compared with competitors?</li>
        <li>Who pays return freight and replacement freight, and is it in writing?</li>
        <li>What is the claim process — inspection, decision time, credit issuance — and who administers it?</li>
        <li>Will the supplier share claim-rate history by product line, so the warranty can be costed rather than assumed?</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <h3>What is the difference between free replacement and pro-rata warranty?</h3>
      <p>A free replacement warranty replaces a failed battery at no charge (possibly with freight conditions) during the free period, then may switch to pro-rata, where the buyer receives credit proportional to the time remaining on the warranty period. Pro-rata compensation shrinks as the battery ages, so two warranties with the same length are not the same deal.</p>
      <h3>What voids a battery warranty?</h3>
      <p>Typical exclusions: physical damage, over-discharge or deep-discharge damage from misuse, charging with a wrong-profile charger, modification, water loss from improper use, and applications outside the battery's design class (e.g. a starting battery used for cycling). The exclusions are where most distributor claim disputes happen, so they must be read before purchase, not after a failure.</p>
      <h3>Who pays return freight on a warranty claim?</h3>
      <p>It depends on the written terms. Many supplier warranties cover the replacement battery but not return freight, which for heavy, hazardous-classed batteries can exceed the value of the credit on a pro-rated claim. Freight responsibility is one of the first clauses a distributor should negotiate and put in writing.</p>
      <h3>How should a distributor cost a warranty into a purchase decision?</h3>
      <p>Treat the warranty as a cash-flow and liability item: the free-replacement window is a stock and freight obligation, and the pro-rata tail is a credit stream. Compare quotes on expected claim cost — free-replacement length, pro-rata rate, freight terms and claim administration — not on warranty length alone. A shorter free window with freight covered can beat a longer window with freight on the buyer.</p>

      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <p>
        Synthesized from warranty practice in the battery trade and consumer-protection background; this
        guide quotes no supplier warranty documents by name and invents no claim-rate statistics — where a
        cost input is needed, the method for obtaining it is given instead.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Battery Council International — industry battery terminology and warranty-period conventions.</li>
        <li>Consumer warranty regulation background — disclosure requirements that shape printed warranty documents.</li>
        <li>IATA DGR / IMDG Code — hazardous-cargo classification that drives battery return-freight cost.</li>
        <li>World Battery Hub lead-acid knowledge — <a href="/lead-acid/battery-warranty/">battery warranty basics</a>, <a href="/lead-acid/deep-cycle-vs-starting/">starting vs deep-cycle duty</a>, <a href="/lead-acid/agm-charging/">AGM charging requirements</a>.</li>
        <li>World Battery Hub manufacturer sub-site — <a href="https://manufacturer.worldbatteryhub.com/verification-criteria/">supplier verification criteria</a> for the evidence a credible warranty is built on.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="muted">Last reviewed: 2026-09-22</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
